1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,120
Backups, downloads, and software almost always arrive as one file that holds many

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:10,727
files. That file is made with tar, and shrunk with gzip or bzip2.

3
00:00:11,197 --> 00:00:12,180
In this video:

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:17,555
archive, compress, unpack, and uncompress files using tar, gzip, and bzip2.

5
00:00:21,575 --> 00:00:24,158
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:24,628 --> 00:00:30,069
Archive, compress, unpack, and uncompress files using tar, gzip, and bzip2.

7
00:00:30,539 --> 00:00:35,983
Two ideas. Archiving puts many files into one. Compressing makes a file smaller.

8
00:00:36,003 --> 00:00:37,964
We'll do both, and undo both.

9
00:00:45,484 --> 00:00:49,858
Here is the tar command we'll use the most. It reads like a sentence:

10
00:00:49,878 --> 00:00:54,061
tar, then options, then the archive name, then what to put inside.

11
00:01:06,531 --> 00:01:10,806
The options are single letters after one dash. c means create.

12
00:01:10,826 --> 00:01:15,447
z means compress with gzip. f means the next word is the file name.

13
00:01:15,467 --> 00:01:18,287
Always put f last, right before the name.

14
00:01:24,757 --> 00:01:28,161
To list an archive use t. To extract it use x.

15
00:01:28,181 --> 00:01:31,585
Swap c for t or x and the rest stays the same.

16
00:01:39,105 --> 00:01:43,406
First, something to archive. We make a directory called project.

17
00:01:43,426 --> 00:01:45,229
mkdir means make directory.

18
00:01:51,199 --> 00:01:57,245
Echo prints text, and the greater-than sign sends it into a file, as we saw before.

19
00:01:57,265 --> 00:01:59,072
That gives us readme.txt.

20
00:02:02,042 --> 00:02:03,456
cp copies a file.

21
00:02:03,476 --> 00:02:09,275
We copy the big system file /etc/services into the project directory.

22
00:02:09,295 --> 00:02:14,673
ls -l lists the folder with sizes. Two files, one of them large.

23
00:02:24,193 --> 00:02:29,037
Now create the archive. Tar -cf project.tar, then the folder name.

24
00:02:29,057 --> 00:02:31,469
Nothing is printed when it works.

25
00:02:35,439 --> 00:02:38,399
To look inside without unpacking, use -tf.

26
00:02:38,419 --> 00:02:41,520
The archive holds the folder and both files.

27
00:02:44,990 --> 00:02:49,001
And the size. About seven hundred kilobytes, the same as the files,

28
00:02:49,021 --> 00:02:52,309
because an archive on its own does not shrink anything.

29
00:02:56,329 --> 00:02:59,765
To unpack, make an empty directory called restore first.

30
00:03:02,735 --> 00:03:05,913
Tar -xf, the archive, and -C restore.

31
00:03:05,933 --> 00:03:11,014
Capital C means change to that directory before extracting.

32
00:03:15,984 --> 00:03:19,270
ls shows the folder came back with both files inside.

33
00:03:23,290 --> 00:03:24,941
One note about this lab.

34
00:03:24,961 --> 00:03:28,214
Compressing with bzip2 needs the bzip2 program,

35
00:03:28,234 --> 00:03:33,089
and it is not installed on this lab image. Without it, tar stops with:

36
00:03:33,109 --> 00:03:34,342
command not found.

37
00:03:37,812 --> 00:03:42,305
So we install it. Sudo runs one command as the administrator.

38
00:03:42,325 --> 00:03:44,451
dnf is the package installer.

39
00:03:44,471 --> 00:03:48,743
Install adds software, and -y answers yes to the question.

40
00:03:48,763 --> 00:03:52,960
Most of the output is download messages; we show the end.

41
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:09,675
Now compression. Take the same command and add z.

42
00:04:09,695 --> 00:04:16,297
-czf makes a gzip-compressed archive. The name ends in .tar.gz by convention.

43
00:04:20,267 --> 00:04:27,223
For bzip2, use j instead of z. -cjf, and the name ends in .tar.bz2.

44
00:04:30,693 --> 00:04:35,560
Compare the sizes. The plain archive is seven hundred six thousand bytes.

45
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:39,309
Gzip brings it to about one hundred forty five thousand.

46
00:04:39,329 --> 00:04:44,263
Bzip2 is smaller still, about one hundred thirty two thousand, but slower.

47
00:04:48,283 --> 00:04:54,471
You can also compress a single file without tar. We copy services to data.txt first.

48
00:04:59,441 --> 00:05:02,674
gzip replaces the file with data.txt.gz.

49
00:05:02,694 --> 00:05:07,634
The original is gone, and the compressed one is much smaller.

50
00:05:12,104 --> 00:05:15,242
gunzip reverses it, and the original comes back.

51
00:05:19,262 --> 00:05:25,663
Bzip2 works the same way. bzip2 and the file name gives a file ending in .bz2.

52
00:05:32,133 --> 00:05:37,959
bunzip2 reverses it, and we have our seven hundred one thousand byte file again, unchanged.

53
00:05:41,979 --> 00:05:45,415
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

54
00:05:45,885 --> 00:05:50,409
Files on disk survive a reboot. We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

55
00:05:53,379 --> 00:05:56,219
Uptime says zero minutes, so this is a fresh boot.

56
00:05:56,239 --> 00:05:59,823
The archive we made is still here, and the checker confirms it.

57
00:06:05,343 --> 00:06:12,151
Your turn. In your home directory, make a gzip-compressed archive called backup.tar.gz of the

58
00:06:12,171 --> 00:06:13,472
project directory.

59
00:06:16,442 --> 00:06:21,694
Then make a directory called lab6, and extract the archive into it with -C.

60
00:06:21,714 --> 00:06:25,489
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

61
00:06:33,009 --> 00:06:35,400
Next up: Create and edit text files.

62
00:06:35,870 --> 00:06:38,772
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
